        "raw": "<p>Let's see... Mastermind and Domain.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I haven't run a Domain villain myself, but I have a few ideas.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>First, from Domain. If Gauntlet is primarily acting through his agents, then presumably they surround him.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>- The World Moves to Defend Me seems like an excellent starting point. There's always an agent ready to step in or take the hit so that he can escape. Maybe&nbsp;something like \"Witness Their Loyalty\"?&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>- The Earth Trembles Around You&nbsp;also works quite well. Use the narrative to craft a situation where the players can only strike at him or at his agents, and you can have this reflect them unleashing a pincer attack and then run off to reload. Perhaps \"They Know Too Much\" or \"Dispose Of Them\"?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>-- Alternately, if he has more plans and contingencies than minions, then This Place Is Mine To Command is a good option. Fill up the environment with his contingencies or powerful allies, and he basically has a plan for everything. Good flavor with Mastermind.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Second, from Mastermind. Should Gauntlet be confronted, then I would say:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>- Villainous Monologue's are amazing in comics, and why should he be an exception? He obviously knows how to 'negotiate', and the heroes are in his place of power. \"Welcome To My Humble Abode\", or such.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>-- Alternately, Contingencies Upon Contingencies gives him a hefty set of bonuses to work with, representing that when the heroes are confronting him, they're still falling right into his traps. \"I've Been Expecting You, Heroes\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>- Exploit Weakness is also neat, and if he's got a good knack or set of gadgets to work with, it's a good way to represent an emergency defensive measure. \"You Aren't The First To Approach Me\" would work for a name.</p>",
